#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/id #uuid "301e7fe6-6892-5e0a-9368-b3857ae8fa39" :question/math-academy-id "e-21649" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228874 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/id #uuid "830924ac-a51c-505a-b5b4-c8a4ad916dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21732" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(x,t) = t\\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_1$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\nf_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1),$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\nf_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_3$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56f45b8-10dd-51a2-b414-43379bcbec60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2(1+t^3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56f45b8-10dd-51a2-b414-43379bcbec60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2(1+t^3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325519} :answer-field/id #uuid "958f7ba9-45b9-5cf0-95b5-ea32ae4d67d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "635c83de-1981-509e-9e67-8590851bc8fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-332944" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229622 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b637dfe-e727-52aa-912d-198aaf72899e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228172} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1c8570-2c6e-5f83-b954-613b0546db61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228173} {:answer/id #uuid "f02be305-f438-50ea-bce8-bf3258137150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228174} {:answer/id #uuid "143ad326-1f0a-57a7-9add-76d500c72637" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228175} {:answer/id #uuid "165aa4ee-318d-5a1a-bec3-372c670de581" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b637dfe-e727-52aa-912d-198aaf72899e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228172} :answer-field/id #uuid "498f5930-328c-5c9a-bfb4-68334fb21da0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba9a4b07-0b33-5dc7-adcc-95cbc6ada4ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-334667" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e3aa6a9-8e85-57ce-bbe3-e4405eabfbd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14t^2+6t^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355548} {:answer/id #uuid "c960e103-028a-55c0-980a-65a14a754183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14t^2+6t^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c960e103-028a-55c0-980a-65a14a754183" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14t^2+6t^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355549} :answer-field/id #uuid "8995fa53-c0a6-5562-809e-9325daeab34e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "91b9ca00-d2fa-58fd-b597-a00f0d7bbfc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334935" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "76fa5adf-5662-5442-bbab-dca086b37914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t^3-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76fa5adf-5662-5442-bbab-dca086b37914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t^3-3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355552} :answer-field/id #uuid "534cf067-0e36-5457-abd5-28af01fb8383" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355551}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4d61795-82bf-597c-8cfb-6a4059b19375" :question/math-academy-id "q-334939" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}+{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =4t({t}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =t(4{t}^{2}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeaf56b4-cffd-5eb1-8362-f76bbe26c288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355553} {:answer/id #uuid "d10f68a2-4eb5-578e-8679-85e5048d40dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeaf56b4-cffd-5eb1-8362-f76bbe26c288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355553} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af3c100-6621-5412-9214-ec5a91babd86" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "50545819-8834-5efd-8519-92619f4fb053" :question/math-academy-id "q-334955" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355555 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35bc3e6f-9cff-5625-914d-d63da0c0876f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^t(1+e^t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35bc3e6f-9cff-5625-914d-d63da0c0876f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^t(1+e^t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355557} :answer-field/id #uuid "1acf0f02-d5d7-5137-8258-e64b60ec45dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "247239c4-d78f-5cb9-9da2-e8f3b6f91f3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334972" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "812b19c3-9f97-576e-9d33-db19495a65af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228237} {:answer/id #uuid "9d78fb6a-32c7-543d-b47c-b8ce2dbd7adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228238} {:answer/id #uuid "e5224786-4fd2-57c1-9c2a-78487764ec64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228239} {:answer/id #uuid "1a473de4-3aeb-5ece-bb11-ac3350abf0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228240} {:answer/id #uuid "79486a1c-11a5-5826-a1fe-8dcb5e902e81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d78fb6a-32c7-543d-b47c-b8ce2dbd7adc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228238} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c938876-c65f-5073-9b79-aac3be6f156b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1cf14fc-3ea5-5845-8a1e-89cdf998a2cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-335237" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf9cee0f-3114-5eb5-ae63-8a55700bcdde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355560} {:answer/id #uuid "befc73bf-d726-5984-b507-9dfcbdf24aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355561} {:answer/id #uuid "7a61897a-3dcf-5042-8a29-53119e41ae61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355562} {:answer/id #uuid "74869bbb-9b63-5446-9f6d-4247153196f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355563} {:answer/id #uuid "884f78c9-d44e-5f4e-b66a-eb347098e6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "befc73bf-d726-5984-b507-9dfcbdf24aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355561} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cffdb50-c759-5ab2-8974-4258e8b2b319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baf1d0ef-7b1b-5759-935f-46c2ba418805" :question/math-academy-id "q-335248" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{|x-t|}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9228306-67f7-58a7-a84d-778cd877070d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355567} {:answer/id #uuid "ad5bdec5-c184-5b33-a9b6-7e7a6a69bbdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355568} {:answer/id #uuid "e907054f-f9da-530b-bc60-b28719bbc9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355569} {:answer/id #uuid "d83f088f-4270-5678-a535-0acf67284a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355570} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbe385d-c3bb-5ec4-9239-b8a851ea45ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9228306-67f7-58a7-a84d-778cd877070d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355567} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de5a58a-9f3b-5ea7-a3bb-16d32491d0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b3c2f68-0bf9-5327-902b-2804c785b8b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335253" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>-1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since ${x}^{2}+{t}^{2}>0$ on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-\\frac{t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ so that $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xt)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>-1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{(1+t{x}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce345467-4d76-5dd2-9f84-cce3be3e53d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355574} {:answer/id #uuid "68436ea9-29dc-503c-8837-3bb304c8884b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355575} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad04d6b-e67d-53ab-9bb6-495c58dc60af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355576} {:answer/id #uuid "4c24db0a-9d5b-51e8-afde-cb28c9532c21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355577} {:answer/id #uuid "21696c8e-a852-5d01-8d44-af2154ac5b0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68436ea9-29dc-503c-8837-3bb304c8884b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355575} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa8349f-d5dd-5428-9870-0f644e840e63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "147a5eb9-ccf5-5722-a03e-4ab0930909aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335255" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
